WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.834 --> 00:00:02.702
Joe: I lived in New York City for years,

00:00:02.702 --> 00:00:05.071
and it has some of the
best food in the world.

00:00:05.071 --> 00:00:07.507
I invited my friend Harry
Kersh over from the UK

00:00:07.507 --> 00:00:10.477
to teach him how we do things
slightly differently here.

00:00:10.477 --> 00:00:13.146
We'll be visiting four
of the top burger spots

00:00:13.146 --> 00:00:15.482
across the city all in one day

00:00:15.482 --> 00:00:18.852
to see if we can find
New York's best burger.

00:00:18.852 --> 00:00:21.321
This is "Food Tours."

00:00:22.155 --> 00:00:24.657
First stop of the day, JG Melon.

00:00:25.925 --> 00:00:28.028
All right, buddy, today
we're having the best burgers

00:00:28.028 --> 00:00:29.863
in New York City. How you feeling?

00:00:29.863 --> 00:00:32.165
I'm excited. Burgers are
maybe my favorite food.

00:00:32.165 --> 00:00:34.401
Joe: Thank goodness. Well, starting here,

00:00:34.401 --> 00:00:36.703
JG Melon. Have you heard of this place?

00:00:36.703 --> 00:00:37.971
Harry: I have not, no. Tell me more.

00:00:37.971 --> 00:00:39.572
Joe: I wanted to start
here because this place

00:00:39.572 --> 00:00:41.741
does burgers traditionally.
You know, like, smash burgers

00:00:41.741 --> 00:00:43.843
and piling it high with
ridiculous toppings, no.

00:00:43.843 --> 00:00:45.979
Here, they serve the double beef blend,

00:00:45.979 --> 00:00:48.948
griddle-cooked burgers. They
do it simple. Cheese, onions.

00:00:48.948 --> 00:00:50.316
We might get a little bacon on ours.

00:00:50.316 --> 00:00:53.420
Simple ingredients. Simple
setup. Going to taste amazing.

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:55.188
Yeah, the vibe in here I'd
say is pretty traditional.

00:00:55.188 --> 00:00:56.689
It's quite low-key, old-school decor.

00:00:56.689 --> 00:00:58.058
Don't accept cards, which is annoying.

00:00:58.058 --> 00:00:59.325
Gotta run out and get some cash.

00:00:59.325 --> 00:01:02.362
But, yeah, I'd say the
menu's fairly simple.

00:01:02.362 --> 00:01:04.964
Classic, nothing too flashy on here.

00:01:04.964 --> 00:01:07.467
I feel like you were pretty
impressed by UK burgers

00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:08.401
when we were touring London.

00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:09.569
Yeah, I was.

00:01:09.569 --> 00:01:11.371
We had fantastic burgers,

00:01:11.371 --> 00:01:13.807
but, I mean, it's next
level here in this city.

00:01:13.807 --> 00:01:14.908
I don't know how else I can put it.

00:01:14.908 --> 00:01:17.077
I'm excited to see what I imagine to be

00:01:17.077 --> 00:01:19.679
a pretty traditional
American cheeseburger.

00:01:19.679 --> 00:01:21.881
Joe: The taste is going to
blow your mind, I guarantee it.

00:01:28.721 --> 00:01:31.024
Joe: Hey, fantastic.
Harry: Look at these!

00:01:31.024 --> 00:01:32.592
Harry: Ooh.
Joe: Let's go.

00:01:32.592 --> 00:01:33.760
Server: Is there anything
else I can bring you two?

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:34.794
Harry: Thanks so much.
Joe: No, that's good.

00:01:34.794 --> 00:01:35.662
Harry: Think we're all good.
Joe: Thanks so much.

00:01:35.662 --> 00:01:37.063
Server: All right, enjoy.

00:01:37.063 --> 00:01:38.731
Harry: They have not skimped 
on the bacon.

00:01:38.731 --> 00:01:39.833
Joe: No, they have not.
They have not skimped

00:01:39.833 --> 00:01:41.568
on the thickness. I mean, 
come on, man.

00:01:41.568 --> 00:01:43.336
You're not going to get anything
thick like that in the UK.

00:01:43.336 --> 00:01:44.471
This is ridiculous.

00:01:44.471 --> 00:01:47.373
The only thing in the UK
thicker than this is me.

00:01:47.373 --> 00:01:48.608
OK. What do you think of that —

00:01:48.608 --> 00:01:49.876
you're getting the American bacon today.

00:01:49.876 --> 00:01:51.277
I'm just, I'm ignoring that.
[Harry laughs]

00:01:51.277 --> 00:01:53.012
You're getting the American
bacon today, too, buddy.

00:01:53.012 --> 00:01:55.081
You got that crispy, thin.

00:01:55.081 --> 00:01:57.083
Harry: I feel like often when
you get bacon on a burger,

00:01:57.083 --> 00:01:59.185
you get the full strips.
Joe: Hate that.

00:01:59.185 --> 00:02:00.086
Harry: I quite like this,

00:02:00.086 --> 00:02:01.521
because they've kind of diced this up.

00:02:01.521 --> 00:02:03.323
So therefore, I guess
when you bite into it,

00:02:03.323 --> 00:02:04.257
you're not just going to accidentally

00:02:04.257 --> 00:02:05.225
tear the whole burger up.

00:02:05.225 --> 00:02:07.093
Optimal eating. Get some
of this onion on here.

00:02:07.093 --> 00:02:08.495
Harry: There you go.
Joe: Come on.

00:02:08.495 --> 00:02:09.562
Harry: I'm putting the pickles on.

00:02:09.562 --> 00:02:11.197
I know you're not a pickles guy.

00:02:11.197 --> 00:02:12.132
Joe: Cheese, look at that.

00:02:12.132 --> 00:02:13.233
It's melted right to the burger.

00:02:13.233 --> 00:02:14.567
It's done to perfection, dude.

00:02:14.567 --> 00:02:17.137
Harry: My God, I'm so excited.
I can't really wait anymore.

00:02:17.137 --> 00:02:17.971
Should we just crack in?

00:02:17.971 --> 00:02:18.838
Yeah, let's crack in.

00:02:18.838 --> 00:02:19.672
All right, man.

00:02:24.477 --> 00:02:27.147
Mm. Mm-mm-mm!

00:02:29.048 --> 00:02:29.883
Man.

00:02:29.883 --> 00:02:31.451
Joe: It's an absolutely fantastic burger.

00:02:31.451 --> 00:02:32.285
Uh-huh.

00:02:32.285 --> 00:02:33.286
Perfect flavor.

00:02:33.286 --> 00:02:34.287
Harry: Did you get that, like, juice

00:02:34.287 --> 00:02:35.989
just pouring out of
that as you bit into it?

00:02:35.989 --> 00:02:38.625
Joe: Oh, look at that. The inside?

00:02:38.625 --> 00:02:41.561
Yeah, that is medium
rare. Perfect. Perfecto.

00:02:41.561 --> 00:02:44.197
The one thing that's
really impressed me is that

00:02:44.197 --> 00:02:47.000
it doesn't feel too dense, I would say.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:48.168
When you're forming the patties,

00:02:48.168 --> 00:02:49.435
sometimes there's too much of a temptation

00:02:49.435 --> 00:02:51.671
to really pack the meat in there,

00:02:51.671 --> 00:02:53.907
and you end up with a
much chewier texture.

00:02:53.907 --> 00:02:57.443
Whereas this is still so
melt-in-your-mouth soft.

00:02:57.443 --> 00:02:58.745
It's really, really impressive.

00:02:58.745 --> 00:03:01.014
Joe: How does this compare
to UK burgers on a whole?

00:03:01.014 --> 00:03:02.649
Good question.

00:03:02.649 --> 00:03:04.450
Joe: Thank you.
Harry: Yeah.

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:06.219
Joe: I'm a journalist.

00:03:06.219 --> 00:03:08.154
Asking questions is kind of my job.

00:03:08.154 --> 00:03:09.422
And the fact that you like that one

00:03:09.422 --> 00:03:10.623
really means a lot to me.

00:03:10.623 --> 00:03:11.991
Harry: High praise. I think one thing

00:03:11.991 --> 00:03:14.761
that's standing out to me
is the way it's cooked.

00:03:14.761 --> 00:03:16.396
I don't think it's super
common in the UK for someone

00:03:16.396 --> 00:03:18.698
to actually ask you how
you want your burger cooked

00:03:18.698 --> 00:03:20.667
unless you're at a slightly
more high-end restaurant.

00:03:20.667 --> 00:03:22.569
So I think that generally
implies that they're using

00:03:22.569 --> 00:03:25.939
fresher ingredients. Everything
is being cooked to order,

00:03:25.939 --> 00:03:27.207
and I think the end result there

00:03:27.207 --> 00:03:30.043
is a really fresh, juicy burger.

00:03:30.043 --> 00:03:32.979
Joe: I totally agree.
It's thick, it's juicy.

00:03:32.979 --> 00:03:35.014
The meat was so perfectly packed.

00:03:35.014 --> 00:03:36.216
It's crazy good.

00:03:36.216 --> 00:03:38.151
Back there, they got
the broiler that kind of

00:03:38.151 --> 00:03:39.786
finishes the cook of the meat, right?

00:03:39.786 --> 00:03:40.853
Before they put the burger in there,

00:03:40.853 --> 00:03:41.821
that's when they add the cheese.

00:03:41.821 --> 00:03:43.523
Not adding the cheese on the griddle.

00:03:43.523 --> 00:03:47.126
It's almost like they're
broiling the cheese to the patty.

00:03:47.126 --> 00:03:48.795
Harry: Mm.
Joe: Genius move.

00:03:48.795 --> 00:03:51.965
So it's almost like
Saran-wrapped with cheese,

00:03:51.965 --> 00:03:53.166
the delicious flavor,

00:03:53.166 --> 00:03:56.102
and also adheres the cheese to the meat.

00:03:56.102 --> 00:03:57.403
Ah, this place, man.

00:03:57.403 --> 00:03:59.339
I mean, come on. I feel like

00:03:59.339 --> 00:04:00.974
I'm in the '70s right now in New York.

00:04:00.974 --> 00:04:03.576
I'm in Midtown Manhattan

00:04:03.576 --> 00:04:05.678
having a big, thick burger, cup of coffee.

00:04:05.678 --> 00:04:07.714
I was going to say, this
place feels even older

00:04:07.714 --> 00:04:10.149
than the '70s, maybe. This
feels like a really retro place.

00:04:10.149 --> 00:04:12.118
I mean that in a complimentary way.

00:04:12.118 --> 00:04:14.320
Very old-school. Retro vibe.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:17.790
Joe: Yeah. I mean, this is
where you go for a long lunch,

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:20.660
couple of drinks, burgers
with the coworkers.

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:22.762
You go back to the office
and do literally nothing.

00:04:22.762 --> 00:04:24.764
Harry: No work. [both laugh]

00:04:24.764 --> 00:04:26.432
This might be the one to beat.
I know we're just starting,

00:04:26.432 --> 00:04:27.700
but, I mean.

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:29.869
It's laid down a gauntlet
already, I will say.

00:04:29.869 --> 00:04:31.437
I know we've got more
burgers to eat today, but ...

00:04:31.437 --> 00:04:32.338
Yeah, yeah, so slow down, slow down.

00:04:32.338 --> 00:04:33.806
This is getting finished
again, man. I'm sorry.

00:04:33.806 --> 00:04:36.042
Joe: That's OK. I warned him.

00:04:37.176 --> 00:04:39.212
Harry and I had some time to
kill before our next spot,

00:04:39.212 --> 00:04:41.914
so we took a stroll through Central Park.

00:04:41.914 --> 00:04:43.916
Harry saw a turtle.

00:04:43.916 --> 00:04:45.151
From there, we made our way

00:04:45.151 --> 00:04:47.287
to another New York burger staple,

00:04:47.287 --> 00:04:49.188
PJ Clarke's.

00:04:49.188 --> 00:04:51.991
New spot, PJ Clarke's. All right?

00:04:51.991 --> 00:04:54.460
It's a New York burger institution.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:55.962
I know there's a lot of facts here,

00:04:55.962 --> 00:04:57.163
but one fact I think is hilarious

00:04:57.163 --> 00:05:00.600
is that Buddy Holly
proposed to his wife here,

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:02.402
and it was their first date.

00:05:02.402 --> 00:05:04.904
What? That's moving a little bit fast.

00:05:04.904 --> 00:05:06.639
Joe: Yeah. I think he was just,

00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:08.875
I don't know. I mean, maybe he knew.

00:05:08.875 --> 00:05:10.310
Oh, also this was Sinatra's joint.

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:11.811
He always sat at table 20.

00:05:11.811 --> 00:05:14.080
Where's table 20? I want to
sit on the Sinatra table.

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:15.381
I think this is it right here.

00:05:15.381 --> 00:05:17.250
No, this can't be the Sinatra table.

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:19.218
So it's like a jazz-legend hangout.

00:05:19.218 --> 00:05:21.354
Joe: Yeah. Dude, this
has been here since 1884,

00:05:21.354 --> 00:05:23.623
which I'm assuming when jazz was popular.

00:05:23.623 --> 00:05:24.624
Sure.

00:05:24.624 --> 00:05:26.392
Joe: Did you notice
outside, there's, like,

00:05:26.392 --> 00:05:28.728
corporate building plazas all around us,

00:05:28.728 --> 00:05:30.630
but this building's almost standalone?

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:31.464
Harry: It's really funny.
Joe: How cool is that?

00:05:31.464 --> 00:05:33.533
Yeah, they really held out,
and thank God they did,

00:05:33.533 --> 00:05:35.201
because, I mean, like I'm saying, man,

00:05:35.201 --> 00:05:37.804
this might be the best
burger in New York City.

00:05:37.804 --> 00:05:39.639
We're going to be the judges of that, Joe.

00:05:39.639 --> 00:05:42.342
Speaking of, which burgers
are we going to be ordering?

00:05:42.342 --> 00:05:44.043
Nat King Cole, baby. We're
getting the Cadillac.

00:05:44.043 --> 00:05:46.012
Harry: The Cadillac?

00:05:46.012 --> 00:05:47.980
Joe: We were getting bacon anyway.

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:49.449
You know, they have other
ones that look like fun,

00:05:49.449 --> 00:05:51.718
but I think bacon's a
nice, [clicks tongue].

00:05:51.718 --> 00:05:52.685
Get that on every single one.

00:05:52.685 --> 00:05:53.986
Harry: I gotta get my fill of that good

00:05:53.986 --> 00:05:55.355
crispy American bacon while I'm here.

00:05:55.355 --> 00:05:56.556
Joe: That's right, that's right.

00:05:56.556 --> 00:05:59.492
You can't get the type of
bacon anywhere in London?

00:05:59.492 --> 00:06:02.061
You can in some places,
but it's way less common.

00:06:02.061 --> 00:06:04.364
Particularly on a burger in a kind of,

00:06:04.364 --> 00:06:06.699
well, it's effectively
like a pub-slash-bar vibe.

00:06:06.699 --> 00:06:07.967
Although obviously with a bit more

00:06:07.967 --> 00:06:08.935
of a fancy-dining aesthetic.

00:06:08.935 --> 00:06:11.270
You can't bring your own bacon to the pub.

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:12.805
You could smuggle some in, maybe.

00:06:12.805 --> 00:06:14.340
Get some pocket meats going.

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:15.208
Joe: With your own ranch?

00:06:15.208 --> 00:06:16.709
Yeah, yeah.

00:06:16.709 --> 00:06:17.910
I like the vibe in here.

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:19.145
It's busy. Popular spot.

00:06:19.145 --> 00:06:20.346
Joe: The checkered red and white.

00:06:20.346 --> 00:06:22.115
Harry: This seems very
classic. Old-school.

00:06:22.115 --> 00:06:24.617
Joe: They filmed some
"Mad Men" stuff here.

00:06:24.617 --> 00:06:25.818
Like, they came here in "Mad Men."

00:06:25.818 --> 00:06:26.652
And that's the perfect fitting.

00:06:26.652 --> 00:06:28.287
Like, getting to work,
drinking all day at work,

00:06:28.287 --> 00:06:30.123
then going to lunch to drink martinis.

00:06:30.123 --> 00:06:30.957
Sure.

00:06:30.957 --> 00:06:32.392
Joe: Before you go back to
the office and drink more.

00:06:32.392 --> 00:06:33.226
Cool.

00:06:33.226 --> 00:06:36.863
And then you get loaded at happy
hour before you drive home.

00:06:36.863 --> 00:06:39.365
You had me up until
the driving-drunk part.

00:06:39.365 --> 00:06:44.170
Drive your giant tank
Cadillac out to Long Island

00:06:44.170 --> 00:06:46.539
to get home at, like, 9 p.m. for dinner.

00:06:46.539 --> 00:06:47.373
Harry: Dreamy, yeah.

00:06:47.373 --> 00:06:50.810
And unwind with two
more glasses of Scotch.

00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:52.712
It's a miracle anyone made it
out of that generation alive.

00:06:52.712 --> 00:06:55.214
Honestly, yeah. How did we not
just go extinct in the '50s?

00:06:55.214 --> 00:06:56.282
Joe: No clue.

00:07:06.526 --> 00:07:09.128
Harry: Ooh-hoo! Thank you.
Joe: Yeah! Oh, my man.

00:07:09.128 --> 00:07:10.263
That's a big burger.

00:07:10.263 --> 00:07:11.731
Joe: Thank you, Eduardo,
I really appreciate it.

00:07:11.731 --> 00:07:12.565
Looks delicious.

00:07:12.565 --> 00:07:14.367
Brace yourself. We gotta talk
about it before we eat it.

00:07:14.367 --> 00:07:15.201
Harry: OK.

00:07:15.201 --> 00:07:19.505
Joe: Also stacked. This
one, thick cut of tomato.

00:07:19.505 --> 00:07:22.241
Lettuce on the bottom. OK? I
like the lettuce on the bottom

00:07:22.241 --> 00:07:24.310
because I know it makes
everything go every direction.

00:07:24.310 --> 00:07:25.912
It's kind of isolated from
the rest of the toppings.

00:07:25.912 --> 00:07:30.016
Very smart, very smart. A
thicker, more smokier bacon.

00:07:30.016 --> 00:07:31.117
This might be the best bacon

00:07:31.117 --> 00:07:32.518
you're going to see all day, my buddy.

00:07:32.518 --> 00:07:34.187
This looks crispy as hell.

00:07:34.187 --> 00:07:37.123
Joe: And you notice onions
underneath for some reason.

00:07:37.123 --> 00:07:38.424
Harry: Are you going to put
your onions in the burger?

00:07:38.424 --> 00:07:39.358
Joe: Yeah, of course.

00:07:39.358 --> 00:07:40.660
Harry: And the pickles on the side again.

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:41.527
You can pickle your own burger.

00:07:41.527 --> 00:07:42.595
Joe: Yeah, thank you,
thank you, thank you.

00:07:42.595 --> 00:07:43.429
Harry: Which I feel like you might skip,

00:07:43.429 --> 00:07:44.564
but I'm definitely doing.

00:07:44.564 --> 00:07:46.532
Burger, some assembly required.

00:07:46.532 --> 00:07:47.333
Joe: Yeah, dude.

00:07:47.333 --> 00:07:49.335
Harry: Hit it with the pickles.

00:07:49.335 --> 00:07:50.169
This thing is huge.

00:07:50.169 --> 00:07:51.270
Joe: This thing is just dripping.

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:52.405
Harry: Mm. Oh, my God.

00:07:52.405 --> 00:07:54.574
My plate looks like the
floor of a slaughterhouse!

00:07:54.574 --> 00:07:56.609
This is perfect! All right, ready?

00:07:56.609 --> 00:07:57.443
I think so.

00:07:57.443 --> 00:07:58.277
Joe: Bite, my guy. Let's do this.

00:07:58.277 --> 00:08:00.112
Harry: Get my optimal angle for bite.

00:08:04.116 --> 00:08:05.151
Joe: Wow.

00:08:06.652 --> 00:08:07.487
Wow.

00:08:08.855 --> 00:08:10.656
Oh, look at the inside there.

00:08:10.656 --> 00:08:13.159
That's cooked perfectly.
We said medium rare, right?

00:08:13.159 --> 00:08:14.427
Harry: We said medium rare.

00:08:14.427 --> 00:08:16.329
Joe: Yeah, perfect.

00:08:16.329 --> 00:08:17.763
Harry: Another place where they asked us

00:08:17.763 --> 00:08:19.799
how we wanted it cooked, which, again,

00:08:19.799 --> 00:08:22.802
I think is a good sign, cooked to order.

00:08:22.802 --> 00:08:23.736
And they nailed that as well.

00:08:23.736 --> 00:08:25.104
Joe: "How do you want
it cooked?" is code for,

00:08:25.104 --> 00:08:26.239
"We're paying attention."

00:08:26.239 --> 00:08:27.373
Harry: Mm-hmm.

00:08:27.373 --> 00:08:29.876
This is so good. This is so fantastic.

00:08:29.876 --> 00:08:31.277
Harry: It's a serious burger, man.

00:08:31.277 --> 00:08:34.213
Joe: The consistency of
the bacon on this one

00:08:34.213 --> 00:08:35.414
might be perfect.

00:08:35.414 --> 00:08:37.817
It's crunchier than normal.

00:08:37.817 --> 00:08:38.684
I like how it's got a bit of bite,

00:08:38.684 --> 00:08:39.752
because everything's so soft.

00:08:39.752 --> 00:08:41.053
Had a few concerns about
the assembly, maybe.

00:08:41.053 --> 00:08:43.689
Because when you have things
like one big slice of tomato,

00:08:43.689 --> 00:08:46.692
big slice of lettuce, and
large pieces of bacon,

00:08:46.692 --> 00:08:47.760
sometimes when you bite into it,

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:49.929
you can pull everything out by mistake.

00:08:49.929 --> 00:08:51.230
But it's really holding together.

00:08:51.230 --> 00:08:52.932
I'm liking the bun as
well. Because somehow,

00:08:52.932 --> 00:08:54.634
despite the amount of moisture
that's been coming off this,

00:08:54.634 --> 00:08:57.069
the bun's holding together, doing its job.

00:08:57.069 --> 00:08:58.271
Joe: It's not falling apart at all.

00:08:58.271 --> 00:09:00.206
Harry: No.
Joe: Oh, wow.

00:09:00.206 --> 00:09:01.240
Wow, wow, wow.

00:09:01.240 --> 00:09:03.376
These fries are nice and crispy, too.

00:09:03.376 --> 00:09:04.277
Harry: Mm.

00:09:04.277 --> 00:09:06.746
I wish the UK did bacon like this.

00:09:06.746 --> 00:09:08.848
I'm willing to admit when the US

00:09:08.848 --> 00:09:10.516
does something better than the UK.

00:09:10.516 --> 00:09:11.851
I think bacon's a good example.

00:09:11.851 --> 00:09:13.352
Because you guys even have
those bacon sandwiches.

00:09:13.352 --> 00:09:15.421
The bacon buddy? Bubby? Buddy? What is it?

00:09:15.421 --> 00:09:16.489
Harry: Butty.
Joe: Butty?

00:09:16.489 --> 00:09:17.323
Harry: Mm-hmm.

00:09:17.323 --> 00:09:18.291
I can't believe you guys are literally

00:09:18.291 --> 00:09:20.660
having bacon sandwiches and nobody's like,

00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:22.128
"Hey, why don't we make
this a little bit crispier?"

00:09:22.128 --> 00:09:22.962
Harry: Yeah.

00:09:22.962 --> 00:09:25.598
Not one person has made that conclusion.

00:09:25.598 --> 00:09:28.534
I wish we would. I've
had too many depressing,

00:09:28.534 --> 00:09:31.537
very pale rashers of
bacon. It's not for me.

00:09:31.537 --> 00:09:32.705
Bacon's a real bummer over there.

00:09:32.705 --> 00:09:33.539
Harry: Mm-hmm.

00:09:33.539 --> 00:09:36.275
What do you think about
the beef patty itself?

00:09:36.275 --> 00:09:38.210
Joe: This one's, like, the perfect size,

00:09:38.210 --> 00:09:41.747
and with all the toppings
together, it's just so good.

00:09:41.747 --> 00:09:43.182
Harry: I think they can only get away with

00:09:43.182 --> 00:09:44.817
putting this many other toppings on it

00:09:44.817 --> 00:09:47.620
because the patty is so
thick and juicy and meaty.

00:09:47.620 --> 00:09:49.922
Not falling apart at all.
It's packed in there, man.

00:09:49.922 --> 00:09:51.090
Which I love.

00:09:52.658 --> 00:09:55.261
I did think that I'm not getting

00:09:55.261 --> 00:09:56.462
as much of the cheese coming through

00:09:56.462 --> 00:09:57.630
in the cheeseburger part of it.

00:09:57.630 --> 00:09:59.599
With this one, the cheese has
less of a presence in there.

00:09:59.599 --> 00:10:00.433
When you want a cheeseburger,

00:10:00.433 --> 00:10:02.401
you really want to
taste the cheese, right?

00:10:02.401 --> 00:10:04.503
I think I kind of come and go on that one.

00:10:04.503 --> 00:10:06.539
I do think that American
cheese is the best cheese

00:10:06.539 --> 00:10:08.941
for a cheeseburger.
Melts without splitting.

00:10:08.941 --> 00:10:09.775
Hear that?

00:10:09.775 --> 00:10:11.577
I also don't personally want

00:10:11.577 --> 00:10:13.512
a ton of cheese on a cheeseburger.

00:10:13.512 --> 00:10:14.780
I know that might sound a bit weird.

00:10:14.780 --> 00:10:17.817
For me, it's more like a
little accent to the burger.

00:10:17.817 --> 00:10:18.651
Joe: Really?

00:10:18.651 --> 00:10:20.753
And almost more of a
textural thing as well.

00:10:20.753 --> 00:10:22.121
Joe: Well, this is amazing.

00:10:22.121 --> 00:10:26.292
I feel great, but we got more
burgers to eat, buddy, so.

00:10:26.292 --> 00:10:28.361
We do. As much as I'd
like to hang out here,

00:10:28.361 --> 00:10:30.896
have a few more drinks,
maybe move to martinis,

00:10:30.896 --> 00:10:32.465
I feel like it's time to go.

00:10:32.465 --> 00:10:35.368
Yeah. Couple more burgers, then
we start drinking martinis.

00:10:35.368 --> 00:10:36.569
Let's go.
Harry: Let's do it.

00:10:39.905 --> 00:10:41.741
Joe: Eh.
Harry: Too fast, man.

00:10:43.776 --> 00:10:44.977
And then I did it too.

00:10:47.647 --> 00:10:49.115
Joe: After that, we
headed over to Brooklyn

00:10:49.115 --> 00:10:52.585
for our next burger,
at The Long Island Bar.

00:10:52.585 --> 00:10:54.120
All right, this is The Long Island Bar.

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:55.888
It's been open since 1951.

00:10:55.888 --> 00:10:57.023
And also, I think you'll
like this place too

00:10:57.023 --> 00:10:58.491
because it has more of an American menu.

00:10:58.491 --> 00:11:00.126
There's cheese curds and deviled eggs

00:11:00.126 --> 00:11:01.193
that I want you to try too.

00:11:01.193 --> 00:11:02.862
Harry: Yeah. Never tried
either of those things.

00:11:02.862 --> 00:11:04.897
They sound like classic
American foods. Would you agree?

00:11:04.897 --> 00:11:05.931
Joe: Oh, definitely.
Harry: Yeah.

00:11:05.931 --> 00:11:07.500
Joe: Also kind of dig the Art Deco vibe

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:09.001
in this spot too, right?
Harry: It's really cool.

00:11:09.001 --> 00:11:10.002
Joe: Like the '50s diner vibe.

00:11:10.002 --> 00:11:11.404
Harry: Yeah, I feel like
tradition has kind of been

00:11:11.404 --> 00:11:13.572
a hallmark of this burger tour so far.

00:11:13.572 --> 00:11:15.541
It feels like we kind of stepped
back into a time capsule.

00:11:15.541 --> 00:11:16.375
I'm really liking it.

00:11:16.375 --> 00:11:18.744
All right. I am dying for
a burger. How about you?

00:11:18.744 --> 00:11:21.681
Yeah, I think I can do maybe
one-slash-two more burgers.

00:11:21.681 --> 00:11:22.982
Joe: Let's do it.
Harry: Cool.

00:11:36.362 --> 00:11:38.431
Ooh, here we go.

00:11:38.431 --> 00:11:40.099
Look at these!

00:11:40.099 --> 00:11:42.435
This is a spread. Dude.

00:11:42.435 --> 00:11:43.469
Joe: Like the American flag in here

00:11:43.469 --> 00:11:44.470
to let you know where you are?

00:11:44.470 --> 00:11:46.038
I'm taking mine out, you leave yours in.

00:11:46.038 --> 00:11:48.207
This looks like a Krabby Patty.

00:11:48.207 --> 00:11:49.041
Oh, yeah!

00:11:49.041 --> 00:11:51.811
It's like the perfect cartoon burger,

00:11:51.811 --> 00:11:54.180
which honestly makes me
want to eat it so badly.

00:11:54.180 --> 00:11:55.514
No time to waste. I'm taking a bite.

00:11:55.514 --> 00:11:57.183
I'm going in. I can't
wait to taste this bacon.

00:11:57.183 --> 00:11:58.684
Harry: I don't want to
accidentally eat the American flag,

00:11:58.684 --> 00:11:59.652
so I'm going to move that one.

00:11:59.652 --> 00:12:01.053
Oh, my God.

00:12:01.053 --> 00:12:04.657
Mm! Mm-hm-hm-hm! Dude!

00:12:04.657 --> 00:12:06.258
Oh, man.

00:12:06.258 --> 00:12:07.927
Joe: You're kidding me with this.

00:12:07.927 --> 00:12:09.729
And with the special
sauce they have on here.

00:12:09.729 --> 00:12:11.497
OK, I gotta go for another one, oh.

00:12:13.365 --> 00:12:14.233
That is crazy.

00:12:14.233 --> 00:12:18.003
This is the secret weapon
right here. This bacon.

00:12:18.003 --> 00:12:19.105
[bacon crunches]

00:12:19.105 --> 00:12:19.939
Harry: How have they —

00:12:19.939 --> 00:12:20.973
I need to know how
they've done this to that.

00:12:20.973 --> 00:12:25.177
Because the shape is amazing,
and the crisp on that?

00:12:25.177 --> 00:12:27.313
Joe: Something that you
don't see in the UK either,

00:12:27.313 --> 00:12:28.481
they batter the fries.

00:12:28.481 --> 00:12:29.648
Harry: Battered fries. That's the most

00:12:29.648 --> 00:12:31.751
American thing I've ever eaten.

00:12:31.751 --> 00:12:32.585
Joe: Yeah, right.

00:12:32.585 --> 00:12:34.620
Instead of it being one thick one,

00:12:34.620 --> 00:12:36.856
two half-thickness ones,

00:12:36.856 --> 00:12:38.524
double it up with the
cheese in the middle.

00:12:38.524 --> 00:12:40.359
Harry: I'm seeing, like,
there's pickles in the middle.

00:12:40.359 --> 00:12:41.961
Joe: Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah,
yeah, yeah, yeah. You see that?

00:12:41.961 --> 00:12:43.796
Harry: They've layered it really well.

00:12:43.796 --> 00:12:45.131
Joe: Sealed in deliciousness.

00:12:45.131 --> 00:12:45.965
Harry: Mm-hmm.

00:12:45.965 --> 00:12:47.733
Joe: Yeah. Oh, this is
so good. And the bun?

00:12:47.733 --> 00:12:49.301
Harry: A bit more texture to the outside.

00:12:49.301 --> 00:12:51.537
Joe: Uh-huh. Mm. Oh, God.

00:12:51.537 --> 00:12:52.772
Harry: This thing's ridiculous, man.

00:12:52.772 --> 00:12:53.606
It's fantastic.

00:12:53.606 --> 00:12:54.974
Harry: Yeah, you can tell
a lot of thought has gone

00:12:54.974 --> 00:12:56.842
into the assembly of this burger.

00:12:56.842 --> 00:12:58.677
Joe: And I like, because it's two patties,

00:12:58.677 --> 00:13:00.780
there's a meat-to-cheese
ratio that's better here

00:13:00.780 --> 00:13:01.981
than it's been anywhere else.

00:13:01.981 --> 00:13:03.883
Yes. Don't let that precious
cheese go to waste, my man.

00:13:03.883 --> 00:13:05.084
Harry: Mm-hmm.

00:13:05.084 --> 00:13:08.154
And the, I mean, the star of
the show. The secret weapon.

00:13:08.154 --> 00:13:10.055
Bacon floss? I don't
even know what this is.

00:13:10.055 --> 00:13:11.757
It's fantastic.

00:13:11.757 --> 00:13:13.392
Harry: Bacon spiral.

00:13:13.392 --> 00:13:15.127
So, I love the American vibe to this.

00:13:15.127 --> 00:13:17.563
The owner is from Wisconsin.

00:13:17.563 --> 00:13:18.564
You know what else is from Wisconsin?

00:13:18.564 --> 00:13:20.065
Harry: Mm-hmm.
Joe: Cheese curds.

00:13:20.065 --> 00:13:20.900
Cheese curds.

00:13:20.900 --> 00:13:21.734
Joe: I don't know if you've
had one of these before,

00:13:21.734 --> 00:13:23.002
but you know what this is?

00:13:23.002 --> 00:13:24.870
It's a ball of deep-fried goodness.

00:13:24.870 --> 00:13:26.372
That's all you need. Cheese, right here,

00:13:26.372 --> 00:13:28.574
in curd form, fried, boom.

00:13:28.574 --> 00:13:30.109
Get in here. Get in here.

00:13:30.109 --> 00:13:32.111
What is that, like, ranch sauce?

00:13:32.111 --> 00:13:34.113
Joe: Yeah, but it doesn't need it.

00:13:34.113 --> 00:13:35.781
It's so flavorful. It's so good.

00:13:36.849 --> 00:13:38.317
He's bringing these in from Wisconsin.

00:13:38.317 --> 00:13:39.952
Harry: Mm.
Joe: Right?

00:13:39.952 --> 00:13:41.487
Really pleasing texture on that.

00:13:41.487 --> 00:13:42.321
Joe: Yeah, it's great.

00:13:42.321 --> 00:13:44.156
You can see why this is, like,
an obsession in the Midwest.

00:13:44.156 --> 00:13:45.791
Yeah, wow. Nice and chewy.

00:13:45.791 --> 00:13:47.459
Not overly cheesy.

00:13:47.459 --> 00:13:50.162
More just like a sort of
chewy, savory mouthful.

00:13:50.162 --> 00:13:51.864
Joe: It's a snack. This is
a Midwest snack right here.

00:13:51.864 --> 00:13:53.666
You can go to town. I
think I need a few more.

00:13:53.666 --> 00:13:55.301
Harry: I am concerned for
the health of Midwesterners

00:13:55.301 --> 00:13:56.502
if this is a snack, but ...

00:13:56.502 --> 00:13:57.736
Joe: Oh, they're fine.
Harry: Very tasty.

00:13:57.736 --> 00:13:58.938
Joe: I don't think there's
really an obesity problem

00:13:58.938 --> 00:14:00.339
in the Midwest at all.
Harry: No?

00:14:00.339 --> 00:14:02.842
Those colorful little
white guys in front of you,

00:14:02.842 --> 00:14:03.943
deviled eggs.

00:14:03.943 --> 00:14:07.279
Hard-boiled eggs, you
take out the yolk ball,

00:14:07.279 --> 00:14:11.550
whip it up with spices,
and reinsert it in there.

00:14:11.550 --> 00:14:12.785
So even though it's a hard-boiled egg,

00:14:12.785 --> 00:14:14.320
it has a softer texture to it.

00:14:14.320 --> 00:14:15.221
Harry: OK.
Joe: And this place,

00:14:15.221 --> 00:14:16.822
usually they'll throw
in paprika or something

00:14:16.822 --> 00:14:18.157
to give a little bit of a kick.

00:14:18.157 --> 00:14:20.492
They have their own house-made hot sauce.

00:14:20.492 --> 00:14:22.261
Harry: OK. Putting their own
little twist on a deviled egg.

00:14:22.261 --> 00:14:23.095
Joe: Yeah. Get in there.

00:14:23.095 --> 00:14:25.097
Why are they called deviled eggs?

00:14:25.097 --> 00:14:26.699
Why are these, like, unholy?

00:14:26.699 --> 00:14:28.634
It's because they're a sinful pleasure.

00:14:28.634 --> 00:14:29.802
Are you joining me on this one,

00:14:29.802 --> 00:14:30.903
or are you watching your cholesterol?

00:14:30.903 --> 00:14:32.638
Joe: No, I'm watching my cholesterol.

00:14:32.638 --> 00:14:33.873
I want to see your reaction.

00:14:35.407 --> 00:14:36.508
What do you think?
Harry: Mm.

00:14:36.508 --> 00:14:38.210
Joe: Yeah, right?

00:14:38.210 --> 00:14:41.780
That hot sauce, man. Nice gentle heat.

00:14:41.780 --> 00:14:44.183
Joe: What their competitive
edge, I think, here,

00:14:44.183 --> 00:14:46.752
is that they're leaning into

00:14:46.752 --> 00:14:49.521
the Americana-ness of it, right?

00:14:49.521 --> 00:14:51.824
A double burger, dripping with cheese,

00:14:51.824 --> 00:14:54.159
delicious sauce, ridiculously good bacon.

00:14:54.159 --> 00:14:55.461
I don't know where this bacon's from.

00:14:55.461 --> 00:14:56.762
Harry: No.

00:14:56.762 --> 00:14:58.030
But I absolutely love it.

00:14:58.030 --> 00:14:58.864
Harry: Mm-hmm.

00:14:58.864 --> 00:15:02.001
Battered fries, battered
cheese curds, devil's eggs.

00:15:02.001 --> 00:15:03.002
And martinis.

00:15:03.002 --> 00:15:04.236
Joe: Yeah, I mean,
Harry: Delightful.

00:15:04.236 --> 00:15:06.972
this is like a niche that
you don't see too often

00:15:06.972 --> 00:15:08.674
in New York or anywhere
outside of the Midwest.

00:15:08.674 --> 00:15:09.675
It's really kind of unique.

00:15:09.675 --> 00:15:11.744
There's nothing like this in the UK.

00:15:11.744 --> 00:15:13.178
I've not had this experience before.

00:15:13.178 --> 00:15:14.647
It's Cobble Hill.

00:15:14.647 --> 00:15:18.117
It's not the tourist center of
New York City, certainly not,

00:15:18.117 --> 00:15:20.286
but definitely worth coming out to.

00:15:20.286 --> 00:15:22.855
Or people who live in Brooklyn, visiting.

00:15:22.855 --> 00:15:25.057
But yeah, this burger's
ridiculous. It's so good.

00:15:25.057 --> 00:15:27.092
It's a thin patty, but it's not smashed.

00:15:27.092 --> 00:15:27.927
Not a smash burger.

00:15:27.927 --> 00:15:29.929
Because we're not getting
that kind of golden brown,

00:15:29.929 --> 00:15:31.864
crispy crust on the outside.

00:15:31.864 --> 00:15:35.100
But I will say it's very well
seasoned. It's very flavorful.

00:15:35.100 --> 00:15:36.435
They did lettuce, no tomato.

00:15:36.435 --> 00:15:37.269
Harry: Mm-hmm.

00:15:37.269 --> 00:15:38.170
No onion in there, either, right?

00:15:38.170 --> 00:15:39.972
I don't think so, no.

00:15:39.972 --> 00:15:43.175
Just a lot of pickles,
cheese, that incredible bacon.

00:15:43.175 --> 00:15:45.744
Yeah. The meat did the
talking in this one.

00:15:45.744 --> 00:15:47.246
Harry: Mm. Which, honestly,

00:15:47.246 --> 00:15:49.014
I kind of think is how a
cheeseburger should be.

00:15:49.014 --> 00:15:49.848
Yeah, I agree.

00:15:49.848 --> 00:15:50.783
Harry: Let the meat do the talking.

00:15:50.783 --> 00:15:51.817
Fantastic.

00:15:52.818 --> 00:15:55.321
Ooh, I'll wash down with
this refreshing martini.

00:15:55.321 --> 00:15:56.555
Cheers, my good man.

00:15:57.589 --> 00:15:58.624
You look like you're enjoying that.

00:15:58.624 --> 00:16:00.893
Should've gotten a beer. Ah.

00:16:00.893 --> 00:16:02.194
All right.

00:16:02.194 --> 00:16:04.229
Killer burgers, place is getting packed.

00:16:04.229 --> 00:16:06.265
Feel like we're taking up
valuable real estate here.

00:16:06.265 --> 00:16:07.766
Got one more spot to
take you. You want to go?

00:16:07.766 --> 00:16:08.968
I do, yeah. Popular spot,

00:16:08.968 --> 00:16:10.536
let's clear out and give them some space.

00:16:10.536 --> 00:16:12.137
Joe: Let's go.

00:16:12.137 --> 00:16:13.739
I wanted to show Harry a place that takes

00:16:13.739 --> 00:16:16.775
a slightly more creative
approach with their burgers,

00:16:16.775 --> 00:16:19.144
so we headed up to Rolo's in Queens.

00:16:20.112 --> 00:16:21.447
I don't know anything.

00:16:21.447 --> 00:16:24.249
I don't know anything about electricity.

00:16:24.249 --> 00:16:26.385
I want to say that. I
don't know a f---ing thing

00:16:26.385 --> 00:16:28.387
about electricity. Like, nothing. OK?

00:16:28.387 --> 00:16:29.788
So we've establishing
I don't know anything?

00:16:29.788 --> 00:16:32.291
I'm pretty sure that's not right.

00:16:32.291 --> 00:16:33.525
Harry: No, that does not look good.

00:16:33.525 --> 00:16:34.693
[both laugh]

00:16:34.693 --> 00:16:36.729
Joe: The f---?
Harry: That can't be safe.

00:16:36.729 --> 00:16:40.933
Joe: No way the electric
company got in the cherry picker

00:16:40.933 --> 00:16:43.302
and got up there and was like, "Yeah."

00:16:43.302 --> 00:16:44.970
"Looks good. Cool, cool."

00:16:44.970 --> 00:16:46.105
Harold.

00:16:46.105 --> 00:16:46.905
Harry: Joseph.

00:16:46.905 --> 00:16:51.310
We've eaten our way through
New York City via burgers.

00:16:51.310 --> 00:16:52.144
We have.

00:16:52.144 --> 00:16:56.415
Last place, here in Queens,
Rolo's, one L, not two.

00:16:56.415 --> 00:17:00.252
Not to be confused with the
very sweet chocolate candy

00:17:00.252 --> 00:17:01.320
with the caramel center.

00:17:01.320 --> 00:17:03.389
Burger here is supposed to be bomb.

00:17:03.389 --> 00:17:04.757
Wanted to end the day here.

00:17:04.757 --> 00:17:07.559
Little outdoor dining. Alfresco.

00:17:07.559 --> 00:17:08.694
How you feeling?

00:17:08.694 --> 00:17:10.029
I'll be honest, not great.

00:17:10.029 --> 00:17:10.863
[Joe laughs]

00:17:10.863 --> 00:17:12.131
I think my eyes ...

00:17:12.131 --> 00:17:13.365
Why?

00:17:13.365 --> 00:17:14.800
I think my eyes might have been bigger

00:17:14.800 --> 00:17:15.934
than my stomach with this shoot.

00:17:15.934 --> 00:17:16.835
Joe: Oh, really?

00:17:16.835 --> 00:17:19.138
As you'll remember, I
was kind of putting away

00:17:19.138 --> 00:17:21.473
the entire burger for the
first couple of spots,

00:17:21.473 --> 00:17:22.975
thinking I was plain sailing.

00:17:22.975 --> 00:17:24.276
I don't recall saying anything.

00:17:24.276 --> 00:17:25.544
No, I don't think anyone did.

00:17:25.544 --> 00:17:27.846
If only someone had told me,
that would've been great, but.

00:17:27.846 --> 00:17:29.715
Yeah, so it's the crew's fault.

00:17:29.715 --> 00:17:31.717
Yeah, I blame everyone except myself.

00:17:31.717 --> 00:17:33.552
Yeah, very smart. Man, you
are becoming an American

00:17:33.552 --> 00:17:34.386
more and more every day.

00:17:34.386 --> 00:17:35.888
Hey, I've made it.

00:17:35.888 --> 00:17:38.590
Joe: So, the house burgers
here, everything is fresh.

